{
  "text": "Selective WebAssembly in AX Code Desktop is not justified by this evidence. The only gated experiment is enabling Pierre’s existing in-worker `shiki-wasm` highlighter. Every other kernel here should stay JavaScript, stay in its current worker, or stay outside Desktop.\n\nHEAD `86fed71500347e9878219c2b98d23abd52d7f8f8`, version `7.30.12`. The excerpts contain no timings, heap samples, or long-task traces. Nothing below is a measured speedup.\n\n## Evidence classes\n\n**Source facts**\n\n- `DiffWorkerProvider.createWorkerPool` forces `preferredHighlighter: \"shiki-js\"` and comments that packaged `app://` workers disallow WebAssembly compilation. Pools are small: unified is `poolSize: 1`, `totalASTLRUCacheSize: 24`, `lineDiffType: \"none\"`; split is `poolSize: 2`, cache `56`, `lineDiffType: \"word-alt\"`.\n- The markdown worker (`createMarkdownWorkerParser`, `createMarkdownWorkerResponder`) parses with `unified` + `remark-parse` + `remark-gfm` + `remark-math`, reuses a live prefix, copies completed UTF-16 text with `split(\"\").join(\"\")`, reparses the live tail, and returns only an unacknowledged suffix.\n- `PreparedMarkdown` installs that MDAST through `remarkPreparsed`, then still runs React Markdown, GFM, math, and KaTeX. `MarkdownRenderBoundary` renders raw text after a render throw.\n- `scoreByFuzzyQuery` short-circuits prefix and substring hits, and constructs a new `Fuse` only for the remainder (`threshold: 0.3`, `distance: 100`, `minMatchCharLength: 2`).\n- `createMermaidRenderer` uses one worker, a queue, an SVG cache, abort, and a timeout that calls `terminate()`. `renderOfficialMermaid` lays out in a hidden DOM node.\n- `WikiGraphPanel.openViewer` calls `desktop_open_wiki_viewer`. It has no local layout kernel.\n- `haveEquivalentPartSnapshots` equates parts with `JSON.stringify(part)`.\n\n**Operator facts supplied with the excerpts**\n\n- Production CSP allows `wasm-unsafe-eval` and does not rely on `unsafe-eval`. The Pierre CSP comment is stale relative to current document headers. Packaged worker instantiation is still untested.\n- `@pierre/diffs` 1.2.11 can select `shiki-wasm` through `createOnigurumaEngine(import(shiki/wasm))`.\n- `PierreDiffViewer.tsx` stops highlighting above `500 * 1024` JavaScript string length, which is UTF-16 code units.\n- Markdown admission is 16,384 code units, with a 1,000,000 cap. Those checks are outside the pasted parser.\n- Streaming coalescing, incremental turn projections, retention bounds, and worker recovery already exist.\n- AkiTTY Web 0.5.2 is the pinned import of akitty `4daf16ce6cbb394a325b0f706779600bba045a83`. Ghostty’s VT parser is already WASM inside that import.\n- Desktop owns UI and control plane. Runtime owns repository intelligence. AX Agent owns durable runs. AX Trust owns credentials and policy.\n\n**Hypotheses**\n\n- Oniguruma may spend less CPU than Shiki’s JS engine on hard grammars, and it may also increase cold-start and grammar memory. Direction is unknown.\n- MDAST structured clone, React/HAST, and KaTeX may dominate `parser.parse`. Unknown.\n- `JSON.stringify` part equality and per-keystroke `Fuse` construction may stall some sessions. Call sites and sizes are absent.\n- Split view is configured as the heavier diff mode. That is a design signal, not a profile.\n\n**Uncertainty from sampling**\n\n`workerFactory`, `PRELOAD_LANGS`, pool failure behavior, Mermaid’s queue/fallback policy after line 65, the official renderer before line 65, Fuse call sites, and the rest of `materialization.ts` were not in the excerpts. Electron/Chromium build and worker CSP response headers were not shown.\n\n## Ranked results\n\n| Rank | Surface | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Pierre highlighter, in worker | Only experiment. Enable upstream `shiki-wasm` after the gates below. |\n| 2 | Markdown `parseSyntax` | Leave on remark in the worker. |\n| 3 | `scoreByFuzzyQuery` | Leave in JS. Reuse a Fuse index only if a palette profile shows a stall. |\n| 4 | Mermaid layout | Leave on the current single worker and DOM official path. |\n| 5 | Wiki viewer | Runtime-owned. |\n| 6 | Terminal VT | Already WASM inside pinned AkiTTY/Ghostty. |\n| 7 | Session materialization | Control-plane semantics. Keep in JS. |\n| 8 | KaTeX, HAST, React render | DOM and component work. |\n\n### 1. Pierre `shiki-wasm` — experiment, not a new kernel\n\n`createWorkerPool` already runs highlighting off the renderer, with AST LRU caps of 24 and 56. The WASM Oniguruma engine in `@pierre/diffs` 1.2.11 stays inside that worker if Pierre applies tokens there. Desktop would change a preference from `shiki-js` to `shiki-wasm`, package `shiki/wasm`, and prove the worker can compile it.\n\nThat is the only candidate whose ABI stays the current worker boundary, whose implementation is upstream, and whose stated blocker is a comment the operator says is stale. It still needs a packaged-worker test. A missing fallback would make highlighting less stable than `shiki-js`.\n\nFiles at or above `500 * 1024` JS characters already skip highlighting in `PierreDiffViewer`. WASM does not change that path. Benchmarks have to include both sides of that gate, in UTF-16 length and UTF-8 bytes.\n\nGrammar output can diverge where Oniguruma accepts constructs the JS regex engine approximates. `lineDiffType: \"word-alt\"` on the split pool must be part of the parity set. Pool sizes should stay 1 and 2.\n\n### 2. Markdown parser — no migration\n\n`markdownWorkerParser.ts` is already an incremental worker protocol. Live reuse requires the same `baseKey` and `request.text.startsWith(previous.text)`. Stable full blocks keep object identity. The responder emits a suffix only when `ownerId`, `baseKey`, and `baseRevision` match. The live tail is parsed again and its old AST is dropped. Finalization clears the cache.\n\n`PreparedMarkdown` depends on a remark MDAST `Root`. `remarkPreparsed` replaces `this.parser` with that tree. A cmark-class WASM parser would still have to be rebuilt into MDAST, then cloned to the renderer, while GFM, math, URL filtering, and KaTeX remain in JS. The transfer can erase a parse win. This cost is unmeasured.\n\n`split(\"\").join(\"\")` deliberately copies UTF-16 units so a sliced string does not retain a streaming snapshot and so an unfinished surrogate survives. A UTF-8 WASM boundary replaces lone surrogates. That changes live-tail behavior.\n\nThe cited stack overflow is in React Markdown render. `MarkdownRenderBoundary` already turns that into raw text. Moving parse into WASM leaves that render path in JS.\n\nAdmission at 16,384 code units and the 1,000,000 cap already bound input. Those limits are the stability control.\n\n### 3. Fuzzy ranking — no migration\n\n`scoreByFuzzyQuery` is a UI ranker: prefix score `-1`, substring score `idx / 1000`, then Fuse. Corpus size is not in the excerpt. Command palettes are often small enough that a WASM call and string copy dominate. A WASM index would also change ranking unless it reproduces Fuse scores.\n\nRepository-wide fuzzy search belongs to the AX Code runtime. A renderer WASM index would be a second search implementation.\n\nIf a later profile shows a main-thread stall, the first change is to reuse one Fuse index across keystrokes while `items` are unchanged. That alternative is unmeasured too.\n\n### 4. Mermaid — no migration\n\n`createMermaidRenderer` already serializes layout, caches SVG text, and bounds a stuck job with timeout plus `worker.terminate()`. `renderOfficialMermaid` calls `mermaid.render` into a DOM container. Diagram layout that needs DOM does not become a numeric WASM kernel. SVG sanitization goes through `sanitizeMermaidSvg` before display; a replacement renderer would have to preserve that.\n\nThe worker/official selection policy is past the excerpt cut, so the fallback’s exact trigger is unknown. The visible design is already bounded recovery.\n\n### 5–8. No-go areas\n\n**Wiki.** `WikiGraphPanel` opens a runtime-exported viewer and queues runtime refresh. A Desktop layout kernel would duplicate runtime ownership.\n\n**Terminal.** Ghostty VT is already the WASM parser inside AkiTTY Web 0.5.2. Desktop should keep the pin and upgrade through akitty `4daf16ce`, rather than wrapping or replacing VT.\n\n**Materialization.** `partSnapshotKey` walks SDK part objects. Those graphs carry merge, streaming `text`/`output`, and ordering semantics (`mergeMessages`, `keepCompletedMessageOnMerge`, `shouldPreserveExistingPart`, `sortPartsById`). Shipping them into linear memory costs another serialization at least as heavy as the current `JSON.stringify`. A mismatch is a transcript bug. Incremental JS projections are already the intended design.\n\n**Render and trust boundaries.** KaTeX and React Markdown emit DOM. Credentials, policy, durable agent runs, and coding tools stay in AX Trust, AX Agent, and the AX Code runtime. Desktop WASM must not become a second runtime or a credential store.\n\n## ABI, strings, and ASTs\n\nJavaScript strings are UTF-16. WASM linear memory is normally UTF-8. Each crossing copies. Markdown, diffs, and Mermaid are string-shaped, so the copy is the payload.\n\nCurrent crossings, from the code:\n\n- Markdown responses already structured-clone `ParsedMarkdownBlock` values, including `tree`. `stablePrefixCount` is what keeps that clone small. A WASM full reparse that returns a new tree every flush removes that identity protocol.\n- Building MDAST from WASM means either JS object calls out of the module, or a serialized tree decoded back into remark’s shape. Both add a second representation.\n- Diff token ASTs are implied to remain in the Pierre worker by `WorkerPoolManager` and `totalASTLRUCacheSize`. That is why Oniguruma is viable: the extra copy is worker-local, into the module and back to JS tokens, and the renderer boundary stays as it is. Confirm that in Pierre before shipping. The pool implementation is not in these excerpts.\n- Mermaid already transfers source in and SVG text out, then caches it. Another intermediate tree adds copies.\n- Fuzzy search would copy candidate strings per query, or keep a WASM index whose lifetime must follow UI list identity.\n- Part snapshots are object graphs. `JSON.stringify` is already the walk. WASM does not remove it.\n\nLone surrogates are the concrete compatibility hazard. The markdown worker copies UTF-16 units because streaming snapshots can split a pair. UTF-8 conversion is lossy there.\n\n## Semantics that a port would have to preserve\n\n- Remark MDAST plus GFM plus math, including reference-definition rewrites that the responder treats as identity breaks.\n- Live versus full blocks, prefix reuse, and “tail is never retained.”\n- UTF-16 code-unit limits: markdown 16,384 / 1,000,000 and diff highlighting `500 * 1024`. A byte-length WASM gate would change when work is refused.\n- Fuse order: prefix, then substring, then fuzzy score. Empty query returns every item with score 0.\n- Mermaid version behavior and sanitized SVG.\n- Shiki token classes and `word-alt` hunks if the highlighter engine changes.\n- Part merge results versus the SDK. Equality has to stay behavioral even if `JSON.stringify` is later narrowed.\n\nVisual or ranking drift is a product change and needs an explicit accept list. Transcript merge drift does not.\n\n## Bounded failure recovery\n\nKeep recovery outside the module and prefer `terminate()` on a worker over a main-thread trap.\n\nExisting bounds to keep:\n\n- Diff pools already return `undefined` when `window` is missing. Highlighting already degrades by file size.\n- Markdown render throws become raw text in `MarkdownRenderBoundary`, which resets when `raw` changes.\n- Mermaid maps construct failure to `MermaidRenderError(\"unavailable\")`, timeout to `\"timeout\"` plus `terminate()`, and abort to the same teardown.\n\nWASM adds compile failure, bad MIME, missing asset, and `WebAssembly.RuntimeError`. For the Pierre experiment, those must fall back to `shiki-js` or to unhighlighted text, and must recreate only that pool. A trap on the renderer thread would sit outside `MarkdownRenderBoundary` if it happens before React’s boundary. Keep any new module in a worker.\n\nMultithreaded WASM and `SharedArrayBuffer` are out of scope. The shown designs are single-threaded pools and one Mermaid worker. Threads would add cross-origin isolation and make `terminate()` a weaker recovery story.\n\n## Deployment and CSP\n\nDocument CSP already has the token Shiki WASM needs: `wasm-unsafe-eval`. `unsafe-eval` is not required for `WebAssembly.instantiate`.\n\nThe open deployment check is the worker, not the document. `DiffWorkerProvider` still records the old assumption that packaged `app://` workers cannot compile WASM. Worker scripts can be a separate response. The experiment needs the packaged worker’s own CSP to compile `shiki/wasm`, with `application/wasm` or an `arrayBuffer` fallback if `instantiateStreaming` rejects the MIME type.\n\nVite must emit the WASM asset into the packaged shell. A successful dev-server compile does not prove the `app://` path. Supply chain stays on the existing `@pierre/diffs` 1.2.11 pin and its `shiki/wasm` dependency. A Desktop-built WASM toolchain would be a new release surface and is not warranted.\n\nA native addon in the Electron shell is a worse fit for these UI kernels: it ties the UI to the Electron ABI, signing, and universal builds, and it pulls control-plane work toward a second runtime. Heavy repository work belongs in the AX Code runtime process the desktop already launches.\n\n## Alternatives, in the order to try them\n\n1. **Measure packaged builds first.** Worker time, main-thread long tasks, cold highlighter start, heap peak. No migration without that baseline.\n2. **Keep the incremental JS already present.** Markdown prefix identity, suffix responses, slice detachment, diff size degradation, Mermaid single-flight, session projections.\n3. **Use the workers already present.** Diff pool, markdown worker, Mermaid worker. Add WASM only inside a worker that a profile shows is CPU-bound after those bounds.\n4. **Batch only where a trace shows a storm.** Mermaid is already queued. Diff pool sizes are already 1 and 2. Streaming coalescing is already part of the product.\n5. **Call the runtime for runtime work.** Wiki, repository search, and coding intelligence stay there. Terminal stays the AkiTTY pin.\n6. **Then, and only then, try `shiki-wasm`.** One variable. Leave `shiki-js` as the fallback.\n\n## Benchmark gates\n\nProposed decision rules, not results. Use a packaged renderer under production CSP. jsdom microbenchmarks do not see worker CSP or WASM compile. Report median, p95, and worst over enough runs that the intervals are visible. Record UTF-16 length and UTF-8 bytes.\n\n**Ship `shiki-wasm` only if all of these hold**\n\n1. **CSP.** Packaged `app://` and web workers compile `shiki/wasm` with `wasm-unsafe-eval` only. A removed or corrupt `.wasm` file falls back to `shiki-js` or plain text. The diff UI stays up. Both unified and split pools recover.\n2. **Parity.** For every preloaded language, token classes or rendered HTML match `shiki-js`, or the difference is written down and accepted. Split `word-alt` fixtures stay stable. The `500 * 1024` JS-length cutoff still disables highlighting on the same strings.\n3. **Time.** On a fixed corpus under that cutoff, worker p95 is within noise of `shiki-js`, or better by a threshold agreed before the run. Suggested rule: require at least 20% p95 reduction with non-overlapping intervals; otherwise keep JS. Cold compile must not push first highlighted frame past a budget also set before the run. Over-cutoff files should be unchanged.\n4. **Memory.** Peak worker heap stays within a pre-set delta of the JS engine. Grammar modules must not grow with the AST LRU. Caps stay 24 and 56, pools stay 1 and 2.\n5. **Kill.** Any CSP failure, any unaccepted grammar drift, or a gain inside noise means the preference stays `shiki-js`.\n\n**Do not open another WASM port unless a profile passes these first**\n\n- Markdown: `parser.parse` is the majority of worker time on admitted inputs after prefix reuse, and HAST/React/KaTeX are separately smaller. Fixtures must deep-equal remark MDAST, including GFM, math, streaming prefixes, and lone surrogates. Suffix identity and `stablePrefixCount` must stay. AST bytes on the wire must not increase. This bar is why a replacement should not be scheduled now.\n- Fuzzy: a realistic palette produces a main-thread task over 16 ms, and a reused JS Fuse index still misses a pre-set budget in that same harness. Ranking permutation against current scores needs an explicit accept list. Large file corpora go to the runtime instead.\n- Mermaid: a headless layout would have to match current SVG closely enough for review and still pass `sanitizeMermaidSvg`, with the same timeout and `terminate()` behavior. Upstream Mermaid remains the owner of that engine.\n\n## Decision\n\nLeave the architecture as it is. Schedule one packaged experiment for Pierre `shiki-wasm` versus today’s `shiki-js`, behind the gates above, with `shiki-js` left in place as fallback. Do not add a Desktop WASM markdown parser, fuzzy index, Mermaid renderer, wiki layout, terminal parser, or session-materialization kernel. No speedup is claimed.",
  "stopReason": "end_turn",
  "sessionId": "01a1a0a0-876d-73f3-8732-12a28c011fbd",
  "requestId": "3c99b471-058e-40fd-9023-981142fc836d",
  "cwd": "/Users/akiralam/code/ax-code-desktop",
  "thought": "Beginning the assessment of AX Code Desktop for selective WebAssembly migration.\n\nThe review will be assessment-only with no implementation. I will independently rank candidate kernels and no-go areas, citing provided file and symbol evidence. Measured facts must be distinguished from hypotheses. Alternatives like incremental JavaScript, workers, batching, and native runtime will be weighed. ABI, string, and AST transfer costs, semantics compatibility, bounded failure recovery, and deployment or CSP constraints will be examined. Benchmark gates will be proposed. Unmeasured speedups will not be claimed.\n\nNo external tools or lookups will be used. The review is strictly confined to the supplied source excerpts and curated evidence.\n\nRepository HEAD is 86fed71500347e9878219c2b98d23abd52d7f8f8, version 7.30.12, Node 26.5.0. Desktop owns the UI and control plane; runtime owns coding tools and repository intelligence; AX Agent owns durable runs; AX Trust owns credentials and policy. Never duplicate upstream runtime or terminal code. AkiTTY Web 0.5.2 is a pinned canonical import from akitty commit 4daf16ce6cbb394a325b0f706779600bba045a83. Ghostty already uses WASM. Production CSP permits wasm-unsafe-eval rather than unsafe-eval. Markdown thresholds are 16384 code units with a 1000000 cap.\n\nExisting source already supports cooperative streaming coalescing, incremental turn projections, retention bounds, and worker recovery. Historical bugs should not be assumed to persist. Files over 200 lines are sampled around relevant kernels, and uncertainty is flagged.\n\nThe first candidate is the Pierre diffs worker in DiffWorkerProvider.tsx. It uses @pierre/diffs/worker with unified or split pool configurations. Unified pools use poolSize 1 and AST LRU 24. Split pools use poolSize 2 and AST LRU 56.\n\nThe DiffWorkerProvider.tsx comment about CSP blocking WebAssembly is stale. The current Electron renderer protocol and web headers already permit wasm-unsafe-eval. Packaged worker viability still needs actual testing.\n\nPierreDiffViewer.tsx degrades highlighting above 500,000 JavaScript string characters, not true encoded bytes.\n\nThe installed @pierre/diffs 1.2.11 worker supports a shiki-wasm highlighter via createOnigurumaEngine(import(shiki/wasm)).\n\nThe Pierre diffs worker is a strong candidate. The WASM highlighter already exists upstream in the library; Desktop only needs to enable it instead of forcing shiki-js. The remaining risk is verifying whether packaged app:// workers inherit a stricter CSP than the renderer document, since the stale comment suggests they do.\n\nAST LRU caches of 24 and 56 indicate AST objects remain in the JavaScript worker heap. Switching to shiki-wasm would not eliminate AST transfer across the worker boundary, since results still cross as structured clones.\n\nThe 500,000-character degradation threshold sits before highlighting begins. WASM migration would therefore not improve performance on diffs that already bail out.\n\nSemantic compatibility between Oniguruma and JavaScript regex engines is a concern. Edge cases such as Unicode, lookbehind, and backreferences may diverge, per Shiki documentation.\n\nThe Pierre diffs worker is ranked HIGH as an enablement experiment rather than a new kernel. Its ABI cost is lowest because the WASM engine already lives inside the worker and strings remain there.\n\nThe markdown parser in `markdownWorkerParser.ts` uses unified with remarkParse, remarkGfm, and remarkMath. It supports incremental live parsing by reusing prior results when the request is live, the base key matches, and the text starts with the previous content. Stable block reference reuse is also present.\n\nThe markdown worker detaches completed substrings via split and join to avoid V8 sliced string retention. Live tail always reparses; old ASTs of tails are not retained. The responder sends only the changed suffix when owner, baseKey, and baseRevision match. It emits one bounded response rather than a transcript cache.\n\nPreparedMarkdown.tsx reuses pre-built MDAST trees via remarkPreparsed. ReactMarkdown still performs HAST conversion, URL filtering, custom components, and rehypeKatex. MarkdownRenderBoundary catches render throws such as remark stack overflows and falls back to raw source.\n\nThe markdown parser is a poor migration target. It already runs off the main thread in a worker and uses incremental parsing that sends only changed suffixes. The expensive downstream work—HAST conversion, React reconciliation, and KaTeX rendering—must remain in JavaScript and the DOM. MDAST is also a large object graph, so WASM transfer costs would be high.\n\nA WASM markdown parser would not reduce transfer costs. The worker already returns a structured-clone MDAST tree across the boundary, so moving that object graph into WASM and back would likely be slower than the current parse.\n\nSemantic compatibility is another blocker. remark-gfm, remark-math, and CommonMark edge cases diverge from standard parsers like md4c or pulldown-cmark. The remarkPreparsed path depends on a specific MDAST Root shape, so replacing the parser would break downstream compatibility.\n\nThe incremental lex cache is JavaScript-specific cooperative state. Porting it to WASM would require reimplementing the incremental protocol or parsing the full text inside the module, which defeats incrementality.\n\nThe split-and-join string copy is a V8 memory heuristic, not a parse bottleneck. WASM would add UTF-8 versus JS UTF-16 conversion overhead.\n\nMarkdownRenderBoundary already provides bounded failure recovery. A WASM trap would need equivalent recovery.\n\nThe 16384 code-unit threshold and 1000000 cap bound work. The parser may already run on the main thread below that threshold.\n\nThe 16384 code-unit threshold is a stated fact, but the sampled parser file does not contain the admission logic itself. Pathological parse spikes are possible, yet the incremental design and worker isolation suggest WASM would only improve latency if profiling shows parseSyntax dominates over rendering. Full replacement is therefore LOW priority; a micro-candidate would require both proof that parsing dominates and a remark-compatible WASM parser.\n\nThe `split(\"\").join(\"\")` allocation is a JavaScript-level cost. WASM would not fix V8 sliced string retention unless the parent snapshot is released, but the worker already retains only stable blocks and the last request text.\n\nThe fuzzy search kernel in `scoreByFuzzyQuery` is a command-palette style filter. It returns all candidates with score zero for empty queries, then applies prefix matching, substring scoring, and Fuse.js ranking. A new Fuse instance is created per call on a small candidate set, and results are sorted and sliced to limit output.\n\nWASM fuzzy engines such as Nucleo or FZF would only outperform this JavaScript path at very large corpus sizes, typically 10,000 to 100,000 items. For the current small palette scale, the overhead of cross-boundary transfer and ABI marshaling would likely outweigh any speedup.\n\nThe fuzzy search function is UI ranking, not repository intelligence. Corpus size at call sites is unknown because the sampled file begins at line 100. `getFuzzyMatchMask` and `partitionByFuzzyQuery` exist, but Fuse.js is instantiated per keystroke and allocates on every call.\n\nBefore migrating to WASM, several alternatives are worth considering: reusing a Fuse index across keystrokes if the item set is stable; moving the logic into a Web Worker if it blocks input; relying on simpler substring or prefix checks that already short-circuit most queries; or using a lightweight fzf-style bitap algorithm in JavaScript for small palettes.\n\nA WASM fuzzy engine would require transferring every item string across the boundary each query, or maintaining a WASM index that must be invalidated whenever items change. Fuse.js uses a unique algorithm, so any WASM port would produce different scores and alter user-visible ranking. Matching Fuse semantics exactly would require custom implementation and extensive test coverage. Without a measured main-thread stall on large lists, fuzzy search is a low priority; replacing Fuse without parity tests is a no-go.\n\nMermaid rendering is already bounded by a single active worker and a shared layout queue. It caches SVG strings and terminates workers on timeout or abort. A fallback path renders directly on the main thread via a hidden DOM container. This architecture makes Mermaid a poor migration target because it already limits concurrency and memory, and its DOM-dependent layout cannot easily move to WASM.\n\nThe sampled Mermaid renderer file ends at line 65, so the worker isolation logic and official fallback selection are not fully visible. The official renderer depends on DOM layout, and while some DAGRE or ELK variants have experimental WASM ports, none appear to be production-ready.\n\nMermaid SVG output is a large string, so WASM would not reduce DOM layout cost. The single-flight queue and timeout-based termination already bound CPU and provided bounded failure recovery. Sanitizing SVG output must be preserved in any WASM path, and semantic compatibility across mermaid versions and themes is an extremely high bar. Rewriting the renderer in WASM is a no-go; Desktop should not fork mermaid. The existing worker isolation is sufficient.\n\nWikiGraphView delegates graph layout to a runtime-exported viewer. Desktop should not duplicate that viewer or its graph kernel in WASM.\n\nTerminal integration is already handled by AkiTTY Web, which imports a pinned Ghostty commit. Ghostty VT is an internal WASM parser, not a standalone product to replace. Desktop should not fork or reimplement the terminal upstream; upgrades flow through the AkiTTY pin.\n\nSync materialization in `materialization.ts` uses JSON.stringify(part) for snapshot keys and merges messages with part ordering and streaming fields.\n\nMaterialization is a control-plane state-reduction kernel. JSON.stringify per part is a potential hot path on large sessions, but merge correctness is critical. Moving parts into WASM would require serializing them first, which would not reduce cost and could introduce merge bugs. Desktop owns this sync of SDK events, so exact SDK semantics must be preserved; any WASM port risks divergence. Streaming coalescing already exists in the current code. This is a no-go.\n\nKaTeX is a JavaScript layout engine, so it is not a candidate for WASM migration.\n\nWASM TeX engines such as tex-wasm are semantically coupled to CSS output, so KaTeX should remain in React. Desktop must not migrate credentials, policy, agent runs, workflows, coding tools, or wiki builds into WASM because those belong to the runtime or upstream. The production Content Security Policy permits `wasm-unsafe-eval` but not `unsafe-eval`, which affects how WASM modules are loaded.\n\nThe stale comment in DiffWorkerProvider claims packaged app:// workers disallow WASM compilation. This is outdated relative to the current Electron renderer protocol and web headers that permit wasm-unsafe-eval. Packaged worker viability still requires actual testing.\n\nElectron workers may need CSP header inheritance for WASM. The wasm-unsafe-eval directive might be required on the worker's own response headers rather than only the parent document. Blob: or app:// worker responses could complicate this.\n\nShiki’s WASM engine uses streaming instantiation, which only requires `wasm-unsafe-eval` and does not need `unsafe-eval`. The production directive already covers this. The remaining gap is whether Electron protocol handlers serve worker responses with the same CSP headers. Cross-origin isolation headers such as COOP/COEP are not mentioned; WASM threads would require them.\n\nShiki’s Oniguruma WASM engine is single-threaded and does not use SharedArrayBuffer, so enabling WASM threads would require cross-origin isolation headers and would likely break other features.\n\nPackaged WASM modules must be included in the asar or extraResources and served with the correct MIME type. The app:// protocol must return `.wasm` files with `application/wasm`; otherwise streaming instantiation fails and falls back to slower arrayBuffer compilation.\n\nDesktop UI kernels should not run as Node native addons. That would force main-process WASM, add deployment and signing overhead, and prevent a universal macOS binary.\n\nEnabling shiki-wasm is a configuration change and asset packaging task, not a new toolchain. The supply chain risk is the provenance of the shiki/wasm binary.\n\nJavaScript strings are UTF-16 while WASM typically uses UTF-8. Every crossing between JavaScript and WASM converts and copies strings. Markdown and diffs are string-heavy workloads. Even inside a worker, moving data into linear memory and back still incurs copy overhead.\n\nA WASM markdown parser would face a serialization trap. It must either build JavaScript objects inside the worker, which negates WASM speed, or serialize the AST to JSON and parse it back, doubling cost. Keeping the AST in WASM and exposing a query API is incompatible with ReactMarkdown and remarkPreparsed, which require a JavaScript Root node. The incremental sync protocol already sends only suffix blocks via stablePrefixCount, so a full AST migration would not reduce transfer volume.\n\nA full WASM reparse would regress bandwidth versus the current suffix protocol. The diff AST LRU lives in the worker, and the Oniguruma highlighter operates on line text inside the worker. The best ABI is likely to keep WASM inside the worker and return only rendered results to the main thread, matching today’s architecture. It remains uncertain whether pierre ships token ASTs to the main thread; that behavior is not shown in the excerpt. Fuzzy search would transfer many strings per keystroke into WASM and read scores back.\n\nA persistent WASM index for fuzzy search would avoid retransfer overhead, but invalidation and lifetime management are complex because the indexed items are JavaScript objects. The `split(\"\").join(\"\")` pattern is intentional to break V8 slice sharing.\n\nWASM UTF-8 conversion replaces lone surrogates with U+FFFD, which would corrupt unfinished surrogate pairs in streaming markdown. The codebase explicitly preserves these pairs by copying UTF-16 units, so passing live markdown tails through a UTF-8 WASM boundary is a concrete semantics incompatibility.\n\nAny WASM markdown parser must exactly match remark MDAST, GFM, and math output to remain compatible with remarkPreparsed, ReactMarkdown, and rehype-katex.\n\nReference definitions in streaming markdown invalidate reuse, per comments at lines 70-71. Fuse score ordering is user-visible and depends on prefix weight, substring index, and fuse scores. Mermaid diagrams carry a language version and sanitizer state. Diff highlighting can diverge between shiki-js and Oniguruma because the latter supports more grammar features while the JS engine uses regex approximations; visual diffs are therefore possible. The lineDiffType word-alt mode only applies to split pools. The highlighting degradation threshold is measured in UTF-16 code units rather than bytes.\n\nA WASM gate must keep the same byte-length threshold as the current JavaScript gate to avoid changing when highlighting drops. Wiki viewer and terminal semantics are owned elsewhere and should not migrate into WASM. The existing MarkdownRenderBoundary pattern should be preserved: render throws fall back to raw text, and the boundary resets when raw content changes.\n\nMermaid rendering can time out or abort, and the worker may terminate. The code defines MermaidRenderError kinds for unavailable, timeout, and render failures, but the selection policy that invokes an official renderer fallback is outside the excerpt.\n\nWorker pool creation can return undefined when no window is available.\n\nDiff highlighting degrades gracefully on large files rather than crashing; the 500×1024 byte threshold is a soft cap.\n\nMarkdown kernels are bounded at 16384 lines and 1000000 bytes.\n\nWorker recovery and retention bounds exist, though old bugs are not to be assumed.\n\nWASM traps such as unreachable or out-of-bounds can kill the worker or module instance. Recovery must terminate and recreate the worker, matching the Mermaid timeout path, rather than crashing the renderer. On the main thread, some older engine traps were uncatchable, though modern WASM catches call-side traps as WebAssembly.RuntimeError. Shared memory corruption with threads could still wedge execution. Single-threaded WASM inside a worker is the safer choice because terminate() provides a clean recovery bound.\n\nA failed WASM parse must not stall the live streaming tail forever; it should fall back to plain text or the JavaScript parser. Incremental JavaScript improvements are already largely in place, such as stable prefix reuse, lex caching, suffix responses, and V8 slice detachment. Further gains from reusing the Fuse index, narrowing partSnapshotKey to dirty fields, and keeping the shiki token cache remain hypotheses for profiling. The existing worker pools for diff, markdown, and mermaid already provide the current concurrency boundary.\n\nWASM inside a worker is an optional second step for main-thread jank. Adding workers and WASM at once would prevent attribution of any performance gains.\n\nCooperative streaming coalescing already exists. The Mermaid queue is single-flight and the diff pool sizes are small. Additional batching should only be considered if profiling reveals message storms.\n\nHeavy coding intelligence, wiki generation, and repository analysis belong to the AX Code runtime. Desktop should not embed a second native or WASM runtime.\n\nNative Node addons in Electron would require per-ABI rebuilds and complicate signing and universal builds. The assessment cannot recommend migration on hypotheses alone. The only near-term kernel that avoids semantics ownership forks is the existing shiki Oniguruma WASM, but it still requires a worker CSP test before enabling.\n\nThe ranking criteria are expected user-visible pain, semantic risk, transfer tax, ownership fit, and existing containment. The shiki Oniguruma WASM inside the Pierre worker is the top candidate for a gated experiment. It is not a new kernel; upstream support already exists in @pierre/diffs 1.2.11. The stale CSP comment is the only reason shiki-js appears in the excerpt. If the pierre API remains identical, the ABI stays unchanged because WASM stays inside the worker. Risks include worker CSP, grammar parity, asset packaging, cold-start compilation, and memory overhead from oniguruma grammars versus the JavaScript engine.\n\nThe shiki Oniguruma WASM must not be assumed faster. It can outperform JavaScript for complex grammars but may lag on simple ones and use more memory, so benchmarking is mandatory. Both sides of the 500×1024 character gate should be tested.\n\nNo other kernel should migrate to WASM in Desktop yet. Fuzzy ranking is a conditional candidate only if profiling shows a specific palette stalling on large N; substring short-circuiting should be exhausted first. Replacing Fuse scores with a WASM index should not be done without concrete evidence.\n\nIf the corpus is the repository file index, the query should route to the runtime instead of WASM in the renderer.\n\nMarkdown parseSyntax is a no-go for replacement. It would only be justified if profiling shows `parser.parse` dominates worker time after incremental reuse on inputs under the cap and the remainder is not HAST, React, or KaTeX. Even then, remark should remain in the worker. A WASM parser cannot practically emit identical MDAST including GFM, math, and lone surrogates.\n\nThis ranks as a low-priority \"investigate JavaScript\" candidate rather than a migration target.\n\nMermaid layout and render are no-go candidates. They depend on the DOM, the sanitizer, and a single-flight worker with timeout already in place, and their semantic surface is too large to migrate.\n\nSession materialization, sync, and JSON part equality are correctness-critical object graphs. Transfer costs dominate, and ownership belongs to the state protocol rather than a numeric kernel.\n\nKaTeX and ReactMarkdown HAST are also no-go. WASM cannot render React components.\n\nReimplementing runtime coding tools, search indexes, or agent loops inside Desktop WASM would violate architectural boundaries. SharedArrayBuffer multithreaded WASM is also a no-go; there is no evidence of need, and it introduces COOP/COEP complications with Electron while making failure recovery harder.\n\nBefore any enablement, the team must profile current production builds on both packaged app:// and web with worker timelines. Concrete benchmark gates should measure main-thread long tasks, worker task duration, WASM compile time, heap peak, time-to-first-highlight, and input latency. These metrics must be falsifiable and must not assume speedups.\n\nBenchmarking must separate cold-start from steady-state performance and exercise both representative and pathological inputs, including existing product caps.\n\nThe shiki-wasm experiment requires two concrete gates. First, packaged app:// workers must instantiate the WASM without relaxing CSP to unsafe-eval; if that fails, the renderer falls back to shiki-js without blank diffs. Second, a fixture suite of PRELOAD_LANGS must verify token-class or rendered-HTML parity between shiki-js and shiki-wasm, though the exact list of preloaded languages is uncertain.\n\nKnown Oniguruma divergences must be listed and accepted explicitly. Word-alt line diffs on the split pool must remain unchanged for a fixture set.\n\nThe degradation gate stays fixed: files with JavaScript string length above 500×1024 still skip highlighting, and the boundary is exactly that limit.\n\nPerformance is a relative gate, not an absolute promise. On a fixed corpus, p95 worker highlight completion must not exceed current shiki-js p95, or the improvement must meet an agreed threshold such as 20%. Cold WASM compile latency must also stay within budget.\n\nThe performance gate must also cap first-paint regression to a fixed millisecond budget. Worker heap peak must stay within current plus that budget, because Oniguruma grammars can be heavier than JavaScript.\n\nFailure recovery must isolate any corrupt WASM asset, compile failure, or runtime trap to the single worker task. The renderer stays up and retries or falls back to shiki-js. Both unified and split pools reinitialize on error.\n\nAST LRU caches of sizes 24 and 56 still bound memory. The WASM kernel must not pin unbounded grammar modules per pooled worker beyond a measured cap.\n\nA kill criterion should apply: if parity or CSP fails, or gains fall within noise, the experiment must not ship. The 20% improvement threshold should be proposed as a decision gate rather than a hardcoded fact.\n\nFor any future WASM kernel beyond shiki-wasm, Markdown parsing would need concrete proof that parse dominates worker time by more than 40% and exceeds a visible frame budget after incremental reuse. It would also require identical UTF-16 lone surrogate roundtrips and deep MDAST equality against remark for GFM and math corpora including streaming prefixes.\n\nMarkdown parsing is effectively a no-go because MDAST identity against remark is unlikely to hold. Fuzzy ranking should only be considered if profiling shows main-thread tasks exceeding 16 ms on realistic palette sizes, and if permutation distance versus Fuse stays within an accepted bound. JavaScript reuse must be tested first and beat WASM in the same harness when N is small. Mermaid layout would require headless layout proof and sanitizer parity; even then ownership remains with upstream.\n\nVitest microbenches are insufficient for WASM because jsdom lacks real CSP. Use a packaged renderer or Playwright against production headers instead.\n\nBenchmark reports must show median, p95, and worst-case latency with a large enough sample to rule out CI noise. No single-run claims are allowed.\n\nAll alternatives must be compared in the same harness: incremental JavaScript, worker-only, batching, WASM-in-worker, and delegating to the runtime when ownership requires it.\n\nRecord both UTF-16 string length and UTF-8 byte count to avoid jsdom confusion.\n\nThe DiffWorkerProvider excerpt covers lines 24–78; the file may be longer.\n\nThe markdownWorkerParser and PreparedMarkdown excerpts appear complete. The fuzzySearch function scoreByFuzzyQuery is fully visible through line 156, but getFuzzyMatchMask and its call sites remain hidden. The mermaidRenderer excerpt cuts off mid-handler at line 65, so queue policy, cache eviction, and fallback triggers are unknown. The mermaidOfficialRenderer snippet only covers lines 65–86, leaving setup before that point unexamined.\n\nWikiGraphView is confirmed not a layout kernel. The useWikiGraph implementation is hidden, but the user’s statement is accepted.\n\nThe materialization file continues beyond line 85; only JSON.stringify equality is visible in the excerpt, so the rest of the merge hot path remains unexamined.\n\nPierreDiffViewer.tsx lines 505–526 and CSP headers are not quoted in the provided excerpts. The user’s statements about wasm-unsafe-eval allowance and stale CSP comments are accepted on trust.\n\nMarkdown thresholds and the AkiTTY pin are also unquoted in the excerpts and must be taken as user assertions.\n\nThe response will be written in English, overriding the default Traditional Chinese setting. It will be a polished technical advisory that avoids contrast framing while still distinguishing measured facts from hypotheses and clearly marking no-go areas.\n\nThe advisory will classify kernels as \"No-go\" or \"Candidate\" using only affirmative language, avoiding contrastive negation. It will not claim to have performed any profiling.\n\nThe source comment claiming packaged app:// workers inherit a CSP that blocks WebAssembly compilation is treated as stale. The user has verified that production headers allow wasm-unsafe-eval, yet packaged worker viability remains untested. Worker WASM is therefore classified as unproven in the packaged app until actual testing confirms it.\n\nPierre 1.2.11 supports shiki-wasm via `createOnigurumaEngine(import(shiki/wasm))`, a fact verified by the operator outside the line excerpts.\n\nGhostty WASM is confirmed inside AkiTTY, and Desktop consumes that pin.\n\nThe markdown worker already returns parsed trees in its response blocks. Structured clone cost for those trees may rival parse time, but that cost is unmeasured.\n\nThe `split(\"\").join(\"\")` pattern copies UTF-16 strings to detach slices.\n\nThe `split(\"\").join(\"\")` UTF-16 copy may appear in profiles for large stable blocks, but that cost is unmeasured. Fuse instantiation inside the search function when a candidate misses its substring is a confirmed fact; whether that allocation matters on large lists is still unmeasured, and call-site sizes are unknown. Mermaid runs in a single worker with timeout termination, while its official path uses the DOM. The materialization file stringifies entire parts, but whether that step is hot remains unmeasured because the file continues beyond the excerpt. In the Diff pool, highlighting preference is handled by the JavaScript engine, and the AST cache lives in the worker options.\n\nThe 500×1024 byte gate is operator-verified against JavaScript string length. The Electron version is absent from the prompt, so WASM feature compatibility cannot be determined without inventing it.\n\nThe wasm-unsafe-eval directive confirms the Chromium version is recent enough to support that token. Testing instantiateStreaming would require a correct MIME type, so that gate should be proposed as a benchmark criterion. Unsafe-eval is not recommended; the production CSP already permits wasm-unsafe-eval, which aligns with the existing sandboxed renderer boundary in ADR-018. Adding native modules would cross a different trust boundary than WASM. Failure recovery for enabling shiki-wasm is beginning to be outlined.\n\nThe WorkerPoolManager excerpt does not show Pierre’s failure mode, so a benchmark gate must force WASM load failure to test recovery. String and AST transfer costs will be treated as a first-class section.\n\nA two-tier ranking is emerging: Pierre shiki-wasm enablement is ranked experiment-only after gates, with medium confidence because library support is verified but worker CSP and timings remain untested; Markdown remark parse is ranked no-go as a WASM replacement, with high confidence drawn from the complete-looking file.\n\nThe remaining kernels are ranked as follows.\n\n- Fuse fuzzy search is a no-go until measured stall; reuse the existing JavaScript first.\n- Mermaid layout is a no-go because its renderer excerpt is truncated.\n- Wiki graph layout is a no-go because ownership sits outside Desktop.\n- Terminal VT is a no-go because Ghostty already runs WASM upstream.\n- Materialization equality is a no-go for WASM; narrowing to JavaScript comparison is possible only after profiling.\n- KaTeX, HAST, and React are a no-go because PreparedMarkdown already handles them in the JavaScript worker.\n\nThe advisory will be organized into eleven sections: executive verdict, evidence classification, ranked candidates, ranked no-gos, ABI and string transfer costs, semantics compatibility, failure recovery, CSP and deployment, alternatives, benchmark gates, and a recommended decision. It will explicitly state that the evidence contains zero measured timings. The markdown threshold is being double-checked.\n\nThe markdown threshold of 16384 code units and the 1000000 cap are treated as operator-supplied product facts. The existing source already includes cooperative streaming coalescing, incremental turn projections, retention bounds, and worker recovery; these are not all visible in the excerpts, so WASM should not be recommended to fix bugs that may already be resolved. PierreDiffViewer degradation is also operator-supplied. The advisory draft is now complete.\n\nThe `split(\"\").join(\"\")` workaround is a V8-specific external-string fix; WASM would worsen surrogate handling rather than improve it. MarkdownRenderBoundary already warns that remark parsing can stack overflow, so a WASM parser would trade unbounded recursion for explicit stack limits. That change would improve failure predictability but not speed.\n\nThe markdown stack-overflow risk is already isolated in the worker, so moving parsing to WASM would not improve stability. The remaining overflow danger sits on the main thread during HAST, react-markdown, and KaTeX rendering.\n\nThe markdown stack-overflow boundary is in the JavaScript render phase, so moving the parser to WASM would not improve stability. WASM compile failure is a new failure mode that shiki-js does not have; enabling it without a fallback would reduce stability, which is why a benchmark gate must force WASM load failure.\n\nWord-level diff kernels such as Myers or histogram diff could be WASM-friendly numeric operations. The lineDiffType setting is a Pierre configuration, and its implementation lives outside the repository.\n\nWord-level diff is a no-go for Desktop-owned WASM porting; the logic lives inside Pierre and should be raised upstream. The configuration splits pools suggest word-alt is heavier than lineDiffType none, but this is a design signal rather than measured evidence.",
